pub4 is governed by MASTER, the primary configuration for every agent
working in this repository. This text is the one contract: every harness
door — `AGENTS.md`, `GEMINI.md`, `GLM.md`, `.cursorlaws`, and
`.github/copilot-instructions.md` — is generated from the marked block in
`MASTER/AGENTS.md`, and `CLAUDE.md` is the hand-written pointer at it. Edit
the block there; never edit a generated door.

Workflow: one shared checkout; commit straight to `main` with path-scoped
commits; never force-push.

Before planning or acting, load MASTER's canonical context:

1. `MASTER/data/soul.yml` attests the identity body and the delivery
   configuration. It may not create or override Laws, precedence, protected
   paths, severity, or enforcement policy.
2. `MASTER/data/laws.yml` is the sole normative constitution. It owns Law
   identities and statements, parent and root relationships, the ordered
   nine invariant nodes, root and severity vocabularies, resolutions, and
   declared enforcement contracts.
3. For unfamiliar or broad work, run
   `ruby MASTER/tools/agent_context.rb --tree` to establish repository
   structure. Use `--json` when a harness needs the machine-readable
   authority, task-contract schema, and capability snapshot.
4. Read the applicable Law records in `laws.yml`, then their implementations
   and tests under `MASTER/law/`. A declared adapter or fixture does not
   prove that the detector ran or that the product is excellent.
5. Read `MASTER/AGENTS.md` for repository workflow,
   `MASTER/docs/task-contracts.md` and the generated
   `MASTER/docs/task-map.md` for task entry, and
   `MASTER/docs/editorial-quality.md` for editorial review.

Read the relevant source, tests, and local documentation for the requested
work. Never substitute remembered instructions, summaries, or assumptions
for the live files.

Authority: laws.yml > soul.yml > Ruby implementations and executable Law
adapters > checks and tree contracts > agent-harness prose. The flat
catalogue is ordered data: one row holds one identity and one statement,
tied to a constitutional node, a root, or a parent. The nine invariant nodes
open the catalogue in constitutional order; the catalogue holds no numeric
priority, no per-law path scopes, and no second precedence graph.

`MASTER/lib/soul.rb` derives its precedence and protected-path views from
`laws.yml`; Ruby constants must not introduce a competing graph or path
list. `MASTER/law/*.rb` holds the executable Law implementations and
adapters. `MASTER/lib/review/scan/` is the scanner runtime — an enforcement
instrument, not a normative catalogue. `SACRED_AUTHORSHIP.protected_paths`
is the one protected-path declaration. Automatic resolutions require proof
fixtures.

One Law system: every Law is one catalogue entry and one `Law.define`, and
its detector is a `detect` regex, a `check` block, or a detector class in
one flat `MASTER/law/`. No compatibility directory or alias covers retired
Law paths. Every Law carries one canonical catalogue entry, a traceable Ruby
detector or an explicit semantic or visual evaluator, and coverage across
lexical, semantic, and visual review.

A Law names the requirement; a detector or assessment names the instrument;
proof is evidence. A declaration, an implementation, a passing test, and an
excellent product are four different claims. A missing or unrun measurement
is INCONCLUSIVE, never PASS. No count of declared Laws, files, tests, or
detectors proves the code correct, the design usable, or the system can
support a stated scale.

`MASTER/docs/synonyms.yml` is glossary-only. `MASTER/checks/checks.yml`
routes operational checks. Neither creates Law identities or normative
policy, and neither overrides the constitution. The four owners are MASTER/
(runtime, `MASTER/bin/master`), RAILS/ (apps, `MASTER/bin/operator` triangle
up), OPENBSD/ (deployment and vm23), and STUDIO/ (Dilla, photograph,
Replicate). `MASTER/tools/` keeps compatibility entrypoints for STUDIO tools.

`law_runtime.yml` remains a live, transitional second metadata source.
`Law::Definition` reads `law_runtime.yml#law_catalogue.entries` for each
executable Law's severity, mode, lifecycle, autofix, languages, surfaces,
and path reach. `LawCataloguePreflight` and `LawRegistryAudit` reconcile
that inventory against `Law.define` sources; boot receipts count it as the
executable metadata population. This is migration debt — not scanner or
runtime settings — and the strict single-source goal is not yet complete.
Do not add normative Law statements, invariant precedence, roots, protected
paths, or global severity vocabulary there. Do not remove or silently
bypass the legacy entries — that would disable or misconfigure executable
Laws. The next migration moves or redesigns this per-Law metadata inside
`laws.yml` and updates `Law::Definition`, `LawCataloguePreflight`,
`LawRegistryAudit`, `BootReceipt`, the DSL fixtures, and all relevant tests
together. Until then, call `laws.yml` the normative constitution and
`law_runtime.yml#law_catalogue` a legacy execution-metadata dependency.

Task entry: before a substantive edit, state intent, repository-relative
scope, mandatory preservation dimensions, and proof. Validate programmatic
contracts with `Master::Fix::TaskContract`; the schema lives in
`MASTER/data/task_contract.yml`. `not_probed` marks an operation that no
run has exercised; permission observations do not prove a successful write.

Shell commands obey the shell Laws in `laws.yml`. The runtime compatibility
registry may still carry older command metadata; it does not override the
normative Law statements. The tree contracts for RAILS, RAILS/master, and
OPENBSD live in `TREE_CONTRACTS.md` at the repo root; for STUDIO work,
follow the project README and source-level contracts. Scoped documents
never replace MASTER. On conflict, follow MASTER and name the stale or
contradictory file instead of working around it in silence.

The HTML preservation guard proves source-level invariants only. It does
not replace rendered, browser, viewport, keyboard, or accessibility
evidence.
